AB  - Seagrass beds are recognized as crucial blue carbon ecosystems, but the variations in their carbon storage capabilities across different habitat conditions are not well understood at regional scale. Following standard protocols, this study examined seagrass cover, shoot density, biomass carbon, sediment organic carbon, sediment dry bulk density, sediment texture and carbon stocks across dense, sparse, degraded and restored seagrass meadows in southern Palk Bay, India. Seagrass covers varied between 10 f 2.65 % and 81 f 4.0 % among the sites with proportionate shoot densities. Monospecies meadows of Syringodium isoetifolium and Oceana serrulata exhibited higher biomass and carbon storage, followed by those with mixed-species. Below-ground biomass exhibited higher carbon storage than above-ground biomass. The total biomass carbon in dense meadows was five times greater than that in degraded meadows. Overall sediment carbon stock in the top 1 m layer ranged from 71.19 f 1.46 Mg C ha-1 in degraded meadow to 113.39 f 9.39 Mg C ha-1 in dense meadow. The estimated quantities of carbon stocks at the study sites are lower than the global mean but fall within the reported range for tropical seagrass ecosystems. The substantially higher carbon storage observed at the seven-year old restored site demonstrates that seagrass restoration can rapidly enhance blue carbon sequestration at local scales. These results provide empirical support for prioritising seagrass conservation and restoration within India's national blue carbon framework, highlighting their direct relevance to coastal climate mitigation strategies and the achievement of India's Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs).
