AB  - Mangroves provide critical ecosystem services, yet they are threatened by the rapid urbanization and land use change. China has witnessed a significant expansion of mangrove areas, driven by the multilevel policies and government financial backings. However, the long-term effectiveness and spatial differences in the investment return, especially in high-density urban agglomerations, remain poorly understood. This study integrates the multiple-source data and economic modeling to assess the cost-benefit dynamics of mangrove restoration in the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area (GBA) from 2000 to 2023. Flood protection, carbon sequestration, and ecotourism ecosystem service benefits were calculated, while the government subsidies and restoration costs were included as the total investment. With the mangrove area expanding from 692 hm2 to 3316 hm2 from 2000 to 2023, the Benefit-Cost Ratio (BCR) improved significantly from 2.10 to 5.98, underscoring the high investment efficiency of mangrove restoration. In addition, the Net Present Value (NPV) of mangrove ecosystem services in the GBA exhibited exponential growth, reaching 283 billion Chinese Yuan (CNY) by 2023, which accounts for approximately 2.3% of the GBA's GDP in the year. Spatial analysis reveals a "core high-peripheral low" pattern for the marginal benefit of mangrove restoration in the GBA. Major cities such as Shenzhen exhibit higher marginal benefits compared with the peripheral areas. These findings provide a quantitative basis for implementing heterogeneous mangrove management strategies and highlight the pivotal role of mangroves as a cost-effective green infrastructure in highly urbanized areas, supporting the efficiency-oriented mangrove conservation strategies for the GBA and other coastal regions.
