AB  - Mangrove ecosystem in Pakistan have experienced significant changes, yet a comprehensive long-term field-based analysis has been lacking. This study addresses this gap by assessing mangrove cover changes between 1988 and 2024 using time-series Landsat satellite imagery in Google Earth Engine (GEE), supplemented with field observation and analyzed using the Random Forest algorithm. To ensure classification accuracy, the error matrix method was employed, yielding an overall accuracy (OA) of >= 93 % and a Cohen's Kappa coefficient (Kc) of >= 0.89 across all study areas. The results revealed that over the past 36 years, mangrove cover increased from approximately 44,486.09 ha to 106,636.64 ha, representing an annual growth rate of 3.88 %. All the study sites: Indus Delta, Miani Hor, Sandspit, Kalmat Hor, and Jiwani and Dasht River Delta, exhibited an increasing trend. In addition, three newly documented sites: Ankara Creek, Darag Hor and Kataho\Shadi Kor, showed a significant increase in mangrove cover, from 48.15 ha to 132.87 ha, with an annual growth rate of 12.69 % during the period of 2016-2024. These results highlight the success of targeted conservation strategies in coastal ecosystems in Pakistan. The climatic factor: positive trend of rainfalls, also supports the reforestation effort. However, despite this overall positive trend, mangrove pattern decreased dynamically at some sites due to anthropogenic pressures: pollution, camel grazing, and fuelwood collection. These findings are valuable for conservation efforts, ecosystems protection, climate resilience, and carbon sequestration initiatives.
