AB  - Despite being classified as a Wetland of International Importance, the Arade estuary (Algarve, Portugal) is a scarcely studied ecosystem. In the past 200 years, it lost much of its saltmarsh area to agriculture. However, natural recovery of the saltmarsh area has been observed in recent years. This study aimed at understanding 1) the origins and pathways of organic matter in the estuary, 2) the general food web organization, and 3) the trophic level of consumer species. Stable isotope analysis showed that the saltmarsh plant Spartina maritima is the primary source of organic matter for estuarine nekton. The food web is dominated by commercial demersal fish, which occupy high food web levels. The maximum trophic level was 2.6, for Diplodus bellottii, Diplodus vulgaris, and Diplodus sargus, seabreams of high commercial value, and an integral part of the local gastronomy. It is argued that restoring of the saltmarsh areas lost to agriculture, and now abandoned, would be important to revert the system to its original integrity, enhancing important functions like secondary production, carbon sequestration, and coastal protection towards sea-level rise, extreme weather events, and tsunamis. This is particularly important in freshwater-deprived estuaries, like the Arade, where riverine inputs of organic matter are very low and, thus, saltmarsh derived organic matter assumes a major role for most of the estuarine organisms.
