AB  - Mangroves, as unique halophytic woody ecosystems in tropical and subtropical intertidal zones, play a vital role in carbon sequestration but are experiencing continuous degradation due to human activities and climate change, underscoring the urgent need for high-precision monitoring technologies to support ecological restoration and blue carbon accounting. To address challenges of insufficient spatial detail, spectral confusion in complex habitats, and patch fragmentation, this study proposes an intelligent mangrove interpretation framework that synergizes super-resolution reconstruction, multidimensional feature selection, and deep learning, tailored for the unique characteristics of mangrove habitats. A 37-D feature set is constructed from Sentinel-2 spectral bands, terrain attributes, and spectral indices, and the enhanced deep super-resolution technique is employed to fuse Sentinel-2 and GF-6 data, generating 2 m super-resolved multispectral imagery. An optimal 6-D feature subset (B6, NDWI, SWIR2, NDRE2, NDVIRE3, and B7) is identified, and a TransCBAM-UNet model is proposed by embedding a Swin Transformer encoder to capture long-range spatial dependencies and introducing a convolutional block attention module to enhance feature representation. Experiments show that this framework achieves an overall accuracy of 98.13%, a mean intersection over union of 83.69%, and a Kappa coefficient of 0.81, outperforming models based on raw Sentinel-2 data and mainstream approaches. The framework effectively reduces boundary blurring and small-patch misclassification. Multitemporal analysis from 2016 to 2023 reveals the overall stability of mangrove carbon stocks, with hotspot clustering and no cold spots, reflecting the persistence of the carbon sink. These findings provide a robust basis for mangrove conservation, restoration, and blue carbon management.
