KW  - CYCLE
AB  - In the surface water of the ocean one finds large regions where nutrients are not completely utilized by the marine biota. In these High Nutrient Low Chlorophyll (HNLC) regions, the marine biology is not functioning at maximum efficiency. This has prompted the idea that biological production in these regions could be enhanced to increase the oceanic uptake of anthropogenic CO(2). This presentation reviews carbon cycling in the ocean and the role of marine biology in this cycle. We address the factors controlling biological production in the ocean with particular emphasis on HNLC regions. Ocean field work has demonstrated that iron is one factor responsible for the failure of the marine biota to utilize the available nutrients in HNLC regions. Iron fertilization in HNLC regions would enhance biological production and potentially increase the transfer of carbon from the surface water into the ocean interior. We present estimates of the potential to utilize the HNLC regions to increase the oceanic uptake of anthropogenic CO(2) and review the potential consequences of large scale iron fertilization on the oxygen budget and the marine ecosystem.
