AB  - Mangrove forests are one of the most productive marine ecosystems in the tropical and subtropical coastal zone, playing an extremely important role in purifying seawater, preventing wind and waves, maintaining biodiversity, and carbon sequestration and storage. Monitoring mangrove resources has important ecological service functions and economic and social value. Conducting long-term monitoring of mangrove resources is of great significance for the research and protection of mangrove resources. This study utilized long-term remote sensing image data from 1980 to 2024, dividing the research area from 1978 to 2024 into five time periods: 1980-1990, 1990-2000, 2000-2013, 2013-2018, and 2018-2024. Six remote sensing image base maps of Pearl Bay in Beilun River estuary, Guangxi were produced from 1978 to 2024, and the area and changes of mangrove resources in the research area were monitored. The main reasons for the growth and decrease of mangrove resources in Pearl Bay area were analyzed, providing data reference for the protection of mangrove resources in Beilun River estuary, Guangxi. The results show that the overall trend of mangrove resources in the Pearl Bay area of Beilun River estuary in Guangxi has been good in the past 46 years, with the number of mangroves recovering and increasing. At the same time, there are also factors such as natural reduction, a small amount of aquaculture occupation, and pollution discharge. This study fully utilizes new technologies such as satellite remote sensing to provide important data support for strengthening the dynamic supervision of mangroves, continuously restoring the ecological environment of mangroves, and promoting the natural ecological restoration of mangroves. It also provides important decision-making support for formulating scientific and reasonable protection and restoration policies for mangrove management.
