AB  - Green tides caused by the bloom of Ulva pose a significant ecological concern all over the word, yet how temperature and density interactively regulate the physiological performance of key species during the biomass accumulation phase remains poorly understood, hindering accurate predictions of bloom dynamics under climate warming. This study investigated the coupled effects of temperature (LT: 15 degrees C; HT: 25 degrees C) and accumulated density (LD: 0.2 g L- 1; MD: 1.0 g L-1; HD: 2.0 g L-1) on the photosynthesis and nutrients removal of Ulva linza during the biomass accumulation phase of green tide. Results showed that under both temperature conditions, the relative growth rate (RGR), maximum relative electron transport rate (rETRmax), the net photosynthetic rate (Pn) and carbon (C) and nitrogen (N) removal decreased with increasing density, In contrast, productivity peaked at high density (HD), a pattern attributed to self-shading and resource competition. When comparing treatments at the same density, high temperature (HT) significantly inhibited RGR, rETRmax, the light saturation point (Ik), and C removal under low density (LD); however, these suppressive effects weakened at medium density (MD) and became negligible at HD. The highest values of RGR and C removal were both observed in the LTLD group. In contrast, neither HT nor HD exerted a significant effect on tissue C content or soluble protein levels. Overall, temperature and density interactively regulated U. linza's growth, photosynthesis, and nutrient removal. Density primarily limits algal growth through self-shading and resource competition, whereas HT exacerbated these effects under low and medium densities but not at high density. High-density accumulation may potentially enhance Ulva's tolerance to warming.
