AB  - Microalgae are receiving an increasing attention because of their potential use as CO2 sequestration method and as feedstock for biofuels production. However, the cultivation conditions of strains should be analyzed in order to enhance sustainability and economical feasibility of this process. Therefore, with the aim to contribute to the development of the microalgae-based processes, in this work, the effect of medium composition and nutrient starvation on the growth of Nannochloris eucaryotum in batch photobioreactors was investigated for the first time. A modified Brackish Water Medium (BWM) and atmospheric air (0.03% v/v CO2) were used. Specifically, the algae growth was first studied using a culture medium where some of the BWM components (inorganic nutrients, soil extract, vitamin B-12) have been eliminated. Then, the starvation effect in terms of nitrate and phosphate concentrations was addressed. It was found that when natural seawater is used as cultivation medium, the growth was inhibited. On the other hand, the algae growth did not results significantly affected by neither soil extract removal or vitamin B-12 addition. To study the effect of starvation in terms of nitrate and phosphate contents, the latter ones were reduced by two and four times with respect to the BWM composition. The obtained results show that the extent of the exponential growth decreases as the initial nitrate concentration is reduced. On the other hand, the maximum growth rate exhibited during the exponential phase was not affected by the reduction of the initial concentration of nitrate. A similar result was found when the phosphate initial concentration is reduced four times. Finally, it was found that the growth rate increases as the pH of the medium is augmented. Specifically, the growth rate was 9.85 +/- 0.54.10(-4) h(-1), 2.03 +/- 0.03.10(-3) h(-1), and 2.86 +/- 0.06.10(-3) h(-1), when pH was controlled at 6.60 +/- 0.67, 8.29 +/- 0.34, and 8.47 +/- 0.53, respectively.
