AB  - Microalgal residues that are generated after lipid extraction are often underutilized, despite their significant energy potential within carbon capture, utilization, and storage (CCUS) pathways. In this work, pelletized and torrefied residues of Nannochloropsis oceanica were converted into syngas via chemical looping gasification (CLG) using natural hematite as the oxygen carrier in a bubbling fluidized-bed reactor. The effects of the oxygen-carrier-to-biomass mass ratio (O/B), torrefaction temperature, and reaction temperature on gasification performance were systematically investigated. The shrinking core model is applied to analyze the gas formation kinetics under the optimal torrefaction temperature and optimal O/B conditions. Increasing the O/B ratio from 0 to 4 enhanced the carbon conversion efficiency from 52.98 % to 70.08 %; however, a trade-off exists in that syngas yield decreases at O/B > 3 due to excessive oxidation of combustible gases (CO and H-2) to CO2. Optimal performance was achieved O/B = 3, at which the syngas yield and cold gas efficiency (CGE) reached 25.78 mmol & centerdot;g(-1) and 53.91 %, respectively. Torrefaction at 200-300 degrees C markedly reduced the tar yield and improved the fuel quality; at 270 degrees C, the syngas yield and CGE increased to 38.12 mmol & centerdot;g(-1) and 64.41 %, respectively. Additionally, raising the reaction temperature from 750 to 900 degrees C further promoted the carbon conversion efficiency (41.62 % -> 68.55 %) and syngas production (16.93 -> 40.02 mmol & centerdot;g(-1)), with H-2 and CO as dominant gaseous products. Apparent activation energies of the formation reactions were obtained as 22.26, 48.93, 38.08, and 21.94 kJ & centerdot;mol(-1) for CO, CO2, CH4, and H-2, respectively. These results demonstrated that torrefaction-assisted CLG is a promising route for upgrading microalgal residues into high-quality syngas while achieving efficient carbon utilization and reduced tar formation.
