AB  - Mangroves play critical roles in carbon sequestration, biodiversity conservation, and coastal protection. However, they face growing threats from a 'two-way squeeze' driven by sea level rise (SLR) under climate change and intensive human activities. To address this, we developed a multi-scenario assessment framework based on the Sea Level Affecting Marsh Model (SLAMM) that integrates high-resolution DEM, land-use dynamics, and regional hydrological parameters. This framework was employed to simulate mangrove migration and habitat loss under Representative Concentration Pathway (RCP) and Shared Socioeconomic Pathway (SSP)-based scenarios, enabling a quantitative evaluation of anthropogenic impacts (e.g., seawall barriers and land-use constraints) against baseline SLR projections. Results indicate that mangrove loss will range from 16 % to 23 % by 2050 and 15 % to 49 % by 2100. During this period, mangroves are projected to primarily migrate toward inland freshwater and saltwater wetlands, exhibiting minimal conversion to dry land. The stability of mangrove expansion is highly contingent upon sediment availability, with sediment-starved areas demonstrating pronounced vulnerability. Notably, anthropogenic structures, particularly seawalls, significantly hinder landward migration by blocking physical pathways and reducing sediment deposition (critical for vertical accretion). Consequently, our simulations reveal that 14.88 % of the projected mangrove loss is directly attributable to the spatial constraints imposed by these seawalls. To mitigate these impacts, targeted strategies to enhance mangrove resilience are further proposed: (1) reducing seawall-induced barriers via ecological modification to restore sediment supply; (2) controlling coastal development to maintain migration corridors; and (3) supporting sediment accumulation to sustain vertical adaptation, effectively addressing the dual stressors of SLR and human interference. These measures are imperative for maintaining mangrove ecological functions, supporting biodiversity, and sustaining coastal resilience under climate change.
