AB  - Accurately discriminating between planted and natural mangrove forests is essential for the assessment of man grove afforestation and restoration outcomes, underscoring a pressing need for developing effective classification approaches and establishing high-quality datasets for planted mangroves. However, large-scale and fine-grained mapping of planted and natural mangrove forests remains challenging due to their pronounced spectral conver gence and fragmented spatial distribution. Despite the global surge in restoration initiatives, dedicated products for mangrove plantations remain unavailable. In this study, we propose a Temporal-Satellite Embedding Fusion (TSEF) framework for mapping planted and natural mangrove forests, which integrates high-dimensional latent representations from Google Satellite Embeddings with temporal features derived from long-term Landsat. Using this approach, we produced the first 10 m resolution map of China's planted and natural mangrove forests for 2024 and reconstructed historical distributions for 2000 and 2015. Our findings reveal that: (1) The total mangrove area of China in 2024 was 31,324.41 ha, of which planted forests accounted for 42.51%, while natural forests covered 57.49%. (2) Mangrove expansion in China exhibits two distinct patterns: afforestation-dominated expan sion (Guangdong, Fujian, Zhejiang) and natural-regeneration-dominated expansion (Guangxi, Hainan, Taiwan), reflecting the synergistic influence of conservation policies and natural recovery processes on mangrove spatial configuration. (3) The effectiveness of mangrove afforestation remains limited, even with the rapid expansion of mangrove areas. Only 26.11% of planted mangrove forests have recovered to the average level of primary man grove forests, whereas 41.68% of naturally regenerating forests reached this benchmark. In conclusion, this study provides a transferable framework for fine-scale planted mangrove mapping and evaluates the performance of Satellite Embeddings, demonstrating its considerable potential for large-scale and cross-regional applications. The produced map offers a scientific basis for quantifying blue carbon sequestration, optimizing adaptive management decisions for mangroves, and advancing the United Nations' Sustainable Development Goals.
